***SPOILER WARNING***


Well, part of what makes wizards so powerful is that they start with
two rings.  Any of:

regeneration
conflict
levitation
invisibility

make the game cake at early levels.

>2) Development over time is sluggish, I have the feeling all I
>ever do is write the E-word. Can't figure out why someone said 
>some time ago that a wizard was easy play.

Wizards require patience and a little planning.  Their main advantages
easily offset their strength/dex/con/hp weakness.

significant increase w/ spell use
cloak of magic resistance
blessed +1 athame for engraving Elbereth
two rings
scrolls/potions/wand

>3) Is an athame an o.k. weapon or just a great BIC. Sting is easily
>aquired while other artifacts elude me. Since my god (neutral) isn't
>exactly Santa Claus, other ways of getting good weapons would be
>appreciated.

An athame's best use is for the E word.  It's okay if you start out with
a blessed scroll of enchant weapon.  Otherwise, you should be looking
for a better weapon.

The 'E' word can be overlooked more with barbarians and valkeries etc.  But
wimpy characters like healers and wizards have to use it often.

Usually, I start of with three goals:  poison resistance/armor/esp

Generally, this is a good idea with all characters, but other characters
can wait a little longer before making such goals a priority.

As for patience, this is mainly setting realistic but necessary goals and
achieving them before doing other things like shop robbing, sacrificing,
identifying BS, polypiling, or whatever.  It doesen't help to have dilithium
crystals, scrolls of charging, or even 10 luck when you're going to die
the first time you meet <tough monster here>.
